				28,
				6
			],
			"Message": "feat(Mist): Implement WebSocket upgrade authentication and fallback dispatch\n\nAdd production-grade upgrade-time authentication to the local WebSocket server, completing the auth contract that was stubbed as a TODO. Native clients (Grove, Cocoon) present the per-spawn 32-byte shared secret via the `X-Land-Secret` header; browser clients (Sky) — which cannot set custom upgrade headers — authenticate via a `?secret=<hex>` URL query parameter or a `Sec-WebSocket-Protocol` subprotocol entry (echoed back as RFC 6455 requires). A constant-time comparison function prevents timing side-channels on the credential check.\n\nThe `HandlerRegistry` also gains optional fallback dispatch: callers may register a `DefaultHandlerFn` via `RegisterDefault` that receives any unregistered method name alongside its params. This lets a single closure forward unknown methods into an existing dispatcher rather than exposing a hard error — critical for incremental migration where some callers still use an older JSON-RPC surface.\n\nTo avoid serialising concurrent connections on dispatch lookup, the handler map moves from `Mutex` to `RwLock` (read-dominated workload — one lookup per inbound frame, writes only at setup time). The `ServeLocal` signature relaxes `Secret` from mandatory to `Option<SharedSecret>` so callers that want loopback-only trust (no auth) can pass `None`.\n\nAcross all source files, doc comments are normalised: remove redundant self-referential phrasing (\"This function performs…\"), adopt consistent \"Builds\" / \"Provides\" verbs, and add `# Parameters` / `# Returns` sections to every public function.",
			"SHA": "abe0b5b5cc2e6257fd238e54a24f11f13cc28319"
		},
